{"name":"Garden of Eden","hex":"#7fa771","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Garden of Eden","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["In the Book of Genesis of the Bible and Surat Sad of the Qur'an, a garden at the source of the Gihon, Pishon, Tigris, and Euphrates rivers, where Adam and Eve first lived after being created."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An idyllic place wherein innocence and perfection reign; any delightful region or abode; a pastoral utopia; an Arcadia.","A state or condition of unsullied innocence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Garden_of_Eden"}],"entries":[{"title":"Garden of Eden","blocks":[{"text":"The Garden of Eden, also called Garden of God or the (Terrestrial) Paradise, is the biblical paradise described in Genesis 2–3 and Ezekiel 28 and 31."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Jan_Brueghel_de_Oude_en_Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Het_aards_paradijs_met_de_zondeval_van_Adam_en_Eva.jpg/330px-Jan_Brueghel_de_Oude_en_Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Het_aards_paradijs_met_de_zondeval_van_Adam_en_Eva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_Eden","wikidata":"Q19014"},{"title":"Garden of Eden (Lady Gaga song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Garden of Eden\" is a song by American singer-songwriter Lady Gaga. It was released on March 7, 2025, through Interscope Records, as the third track from Gaga's studio album, Mayhem (2025). It was written and produced jointly by Gaga, Andrew Watt, Cirkut, and Gesaffelstein. \"Garden of Eden\" is an electro, electroclash, synth-pop, and dance-pop track whose lyrics describe a one-night adventure at a rave, using the Garden of Eden as a reference to paradise."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_Eden_(Lady_Gaga_song)","wikidata":"Q133163858"},{"title":"Garden","blocks":[{"text":"A garden is a planned space, usually outdoors, set aside for the cultivation, display, and enjoyment of plants and other forms of nature. The single feature identifying even the wildest wild garden is control. The garden can incorporate both natural and artificial materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden","wikidata":"Q1107656"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Expulsion from Paradise, painting by James Tissot, c. 1896–1902","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJames_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Adam_and_Eve_Driven_From_Paradise_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/CaedmonManuscriptPage46Illust.jpg/500px-CaedmonManuscriptPage46Illust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/CaedmonManuscriptPage46Illust.jpg/1280px-Caedmo